\title{Surplus Calculus for Union-Closed Families:\\
Adjoining the Top, Averaging Criteria,\\ and a Sharp Local Degree Law}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Let $F$ be a finite family of finite sets, let $\deeg_F(x)$ be the number of members of $F$
containing $x$, and call $x$ \emph{abundant} in $F$ when $|F| \le 2\deeg_F(x)$. Abundance is the
notion appearing in Frankl's union-closed sets conjecture. We study the operation
$F \mapsto F^{+} := F \cup \{\Top F\}$ of adjoining the \emph{top} $\Top F = \bigcup_{A\in F} A$,
the first step of the union closure, and develop an exact accounting --- the \emph{surplus
calculus} --- for how adjunctions move the abundance threshold. We prove: (i) if $x$ is abundant
in $F$ and $x \in \Top F$ then $x$ is abundant in $F^{+}$, the surplus
$\sigma_F(x) = 2\deeg_F(x) - |F|$ rising by exactly $1$ when the top is new; the parity of $|F|$
is not an obstruction but a free strengthening, and for an abundant $x$ abundance survives
adjoining the top \emph{if and only if} $F \ne \varnothing$; one step further --- pairwise
completion or the full closure --- abundance can be destroyed. (ii) Via the double count
$\sum_{x\in s}\deeg_F(x) = \sum_{A\in F}|A|$, an averaging hypothesis $|s|\,|F| \le 2\sum_{A\in F}|A|$
on a nonempty ground set $s$ containing all members forces some element of $s$ to be abundant,
with no union-closedness assumed; this criterion is itself preserved by adjoining the top, and
the operation strictly increases the density $\deeg_F(x)/|F|$ whenever the top is new and $x$
misses some member. (iii) For union-closed $F$ with $a \in A \in F$ one has
$|F| \le (2^{|A|-1}+1)\deeg_F(a)$, and the constant is attained for every $|A|$, so no argument
using a single member of size $\ge 2$ can prove Frankl's conjecture. (iv) Frankl's conjecture is
proved here for families containing a singleton or a pair, for chains of arbitrary length, for
families with at most four members, for families satisfying the averaging criterion, and for all
families over a three-element ground set; and the existence of an abundant element of a nonempty
ground set $s$ is equivalent to the single test $|F| \le 2\max_{x\in s}\deeg_F(x)$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A finite family $F$ of finite sets is \emph{union-closed} if $A \cup B \in F$ whenever
$A, B \in F$. In 1979 P\'eter Frankl conjectured that every union-closed family with at least one
nonempty member has an element lying in at least half of its members. Despite its elementary
statement the conjecture is open; what is known consists largely of unconditional special cases and
of bounds on the best guaranteed fraction.

This paper does not resolve the conjecture. It studies exactly and completely one primitive
operation on families --- adjoining the top --- and extracts a bookkeeping device that explains why
that operation is always safe, predicts precisely when larger closure steps are dangerous, and
interacts consistently with a global averaging criterion for the existence of abundant elements. We
also prove a sharp local degree law that rules out an entire class of proof strategies.

\section{Definitions}

\begin{definition}[Degree]
For a family $F$ and an element $x$, $\deeg_F(x) := \#\{A \in F : x \in A\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Abundance]
$x$ is \emph{abundant} in $F$ if $|F| \le 2\deeg_F(x)$; equivalently, $x$ lies in at least half of
the members. Every $x$ is vacuously abundant in the empty family.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Top; adjoining the top]
$\Top F := \bigcup_{A\in F} A$, and $F^{+} := F \cup \{\Top F\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Surplus]
$\sigma_F(x) := 2\deeg_F(x) - |F| \in \mathbb{Z}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Union closure; pairwise completion]
$\ucl(F)$ is the family of all unions $\Top S$ over nonempty subfamilies $S \subseteq F$;
$\pu(F) := F \cup \{A\cup B : A,B \in F\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Total size; density]
$T(F) := \sum_{A\in F}|A|$, the number of incidences $(x,A)$ with $x \in A \in F$; and, for
$F \ne \varnothing$, $d_F(x) := \deeg_F(x)/|F| \in [0,1]$.
\end{definition}

Two facts are used throughout: $\deeg_F(x) \le |F|$, and the partition of $F$ into members
containing $x$ and members avoiding $x$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:partition}
  \deeg_F(x) + \#\{A \in F : x \notin A\} = |F|.
\end{equation}

\begin{proposition}[Abundance as a sign condition]
$x$ is abundant in $F$ if and only if $\sigma_F(x) \ge 0$.
\end{proposition}

\section{The surplus calculus}

\begin{lemma}[Unit steps]\label{lem:unit}
Let $A \notin F$. If $x \in A$ then $\sigma_{F\cup\{A\}}(x) = \sigma_F(x) + 1$; if $x \notin A$ then
$\sigma_{F\cup\{A\}}(x) = \sigma_F(x) - 1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
In the first case both $|F|$ and $\deeg_F(x)$ increase by exactly $1$, so $\sigma$ changes by
$2\cdot 1 - 1 = +1$. In the second, $|F|$ increases by $1$ while the degree is unchanged, so
$\sigma$ changes by $-1$.
\end{proof}

This is the \emph{two-versus-one accounting}: a new member is charged once against the family size
but pays twice into the degree, provided it contains $x$.

\begin{lemma}[Additivity over disjoint batches]\label{lem:add}
If $F$ and $G$ are disjoint families then $\deeg_{F\cup G}(x) = \deeg_F(x) + \deeg_G(x)$ and
$\sigma_{F\cup G}(x) = \sigma_F(x) + \sigma_G(x)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Filtering commutes with disjoint union and cardinality is additive on disjoint unions; the surplus
identity follows by linearity.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Batch stability]\label{cor:batch}
Let $F,G$ be disjoint. If $x$ is abundant in $F$ and at least half of the sets in $G$ contain $x$
(equivalently $\sigma_G(x) \ge 0$), then $x$ is abundant in $F \cup G$.
\end{corollary}

For a singleton batch, $\sigma_{\{A\}}(x) = +1$ if $x \in A$ and $-1$ otherwise. Adjoining the top
is the batch of size one with surplus $+1$.

\section{Adjoining the top}

\begin{lemma}[The top is stable]\label{lem:topstable}
$\Top{F^{+}} = \Top F$ and $(F^{+})^{+} = F^{+}$; moreover $F \subseteq F^{+}$ and $F^{+}$ is
always nonempty.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Union-closed families own their top]\label{lem:own}
If $F$ is union-closed and $S \subseteq F$ is nonempty then $\Top S \in F$. In particular a nonempty
union-closed family satisfies $F^{+} = F$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Induction on the nonempty finite subfamily $S$: a singleton $\{A\}$ has $\Top S = A \in F$, and for
$S = \{A\}\sqcup S'$ we get $\Top S = A \cup \Top{S'} \in F$ by the inductive hypothesis and
union-closedness. Apply with $S = F$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Redundancy of the membership hypothesis]\label{lem:redundant}
If $F$ is nonempty and $x$ is abundant in $F$, then $x \in \Top F$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$|F| \ge 1$ and $|F| \le 2\deeg_F(x)$ force $\deeg_F(x) \ge 1$, so some member contains $x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Main theorem: adjoining the top preserves a witness]\label{thm:main}
Let $x$ be abundant in $F$ with $x \in \Top F$. Then $x$ is abundant in $F^{+}$; and if
$\Top F \notin F$ then $\sigma_{F^{+}}(x) = \sigma_F(x) + 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $\Top F \in F$ then $F^{+} = F$. Otherwise apply Lemma~\ref{lem:unit} with $A = \Top F$, which
contains $x$: the surplus rises by $1$, and a nonnegative surplus stays nonnegative.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:ne}
If $F$ is nonempty and $x$ is abundant in $F$, then $x$ is abundant in $F^{+}$. More generally,
adjoining any set containing $x$ preserves abundance of $x$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Exact boundary]\label{thm:boundary}
Let $x$ be abundant in $F$. Then $x$ is abundant in $F^{+}$ if and only if $F \ne \varnothing$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Sufficiency is Corollary~\ref{cor:ne}. Conversely, if $F = \varnothing$ then $\Top F = \varnothing$
and $F^{+} = \{\varnothing\}$, so $|F^{+}| = 1 > 0 = 2\deeg_{F^{+}}(x)$.
\end{proof}

Thus $F = \varnothing$ is the unique counterexample to the unguarded claim, and nonemptiness is
exactly the additional hypothesis required.

\begin{theorem}[Parity is a bonus, not an obstruction]\label{thm:parity}
If $|F|$ is odd and $x$ is abundant in $F$, then $|F| + 1 \le 2\deeg_F(x)$: abundance on odd
families is automatically strict.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $|F| = 2k+1$; then $2k+1 \le 2\deeg_F(x)$ with an even right-hand side forces
$2k+2 \le 2\deeg_F(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Monotonicity]
If $x \in \Top F$ then $\sigma_F(x) \le \sigma_{F^{+}}(x)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[The adjoined set must contain $x$]\label{prop:sharp1}
There are a family $F$, a set $A$ and an element $x$ with $x$ abundant in $F$, $x \notin A$, and $x$
not abundant in $F \cup \{A\}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Over the ground set $\{0,1\}$ take $F = \{\varnothing, \{0\}\}$, $A = \{1\}$, $x = 0$. Then
$|F| = 2$, $\deeg_F(0)=1$, so $\sigma_F(0) = 0$; after adjoining $A$, $|F\cup\{A\}| = 3$ with degree
still $1$, so $\sigma = -1$.
\end{proof}

\section{One step further: pairwise completion and the closure}

\begin{proposition}[Closure basics]
$\ucl(F)$ is union-closed, contains $F$, and is contained in every union-closed family containing
$F$; hence $\ucl(F) = F$ iff $F$ is union-closed. If $F \ne \varnothing$ then $\Top F \in \ucl(F)$,
so $F^{+} \subseteq \ucl(F)$; also $\pu(F) \subseteq \ucl(F)$, and $\pu(F) = F$ iff $F$ is
union-closed.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[The boundary is sharp at one set]\label{thm:sharpclosure}
There exist a nonempty family $F$ and an element $x$ such that $x$ is abundant in $F$ and in
$F^{+}$, but not in $\pu(F)$ and not in $\ucl(F)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Over $\{0,1,2\}$ take $F = \{\{0,1,2\},\{0,1\},\{1\},\{2\}\}$ and $x = 0$. Then $|F| = 4$ and
$\deeg_F(0) = 2$, so $x$ is abundant, and $\Top F = \{0,1,2\} \in F$ gives $F^{+} = F$. One pairwise
step adds $\{1\}\cup\{2\} = \{1,2\}$, which avoids $0$: now five members with degree $2$, so
$\sigma = -1$. The full closure is $\{\{1\},\{2\},\{1,2\},\{0,1\},\{0,1,2\}\}$, again five members
of which two contain $0$.
\end{proof}

Abundance is therefore not monotone along the closure: each adjunction is worth exactly $\pm 1$ to a
fixed element, and the closure interleaves good and bad steps.

\section{Creating abundance: double counting and an averaging criterion}

\begin{theorem}[Double counting]\label{thm:double}
Let $s$ be finite with $A \subseteq s$ for every $A \in F$. Then
$\sum_{x\in s}\deeg_F(x) = \sum_{A\in F}|A| = T(F)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $\deeg_F(x) = \sum_{A\in F}[x \in A]$ and exchange the order of summation, obtaining
$\sum_{A\in F} \#(A\cap s) = \sum_{A\in F}|A|$ since $A \subseteq s$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Averaging criterion]\label{thm:avg}
Let $s$ be nonempty with $A \subseteq s$ for all $A \in F$, and suppose $|s|\,|F| \le 2T(F)$, i.e.\
the members of $F$ have average size at least $|s|/2$. Then some $x \in s$ is abundant in $F$. No
union-closedness is assumed.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Otherwise $2\deeg_F(x) < |F|$ for every $x \in s$; summing this strict inequality over the nonempty
set $s$ gives $2\sum_{x\in s}\deeg_F(x) < |s|\,|F|$, and the left-hand side is $2T(F)$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:double}, contradicting the hypothesis.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Sufficient but not necessary]\label{prop:notnec}
The union-closed family $F = \{\varnothing,\{0\},\{1\},\{0,1\},\{0,1,2\}\}$ has the abundant element
$0$ (degree $3$ of $5$), yet $T(F) = 7$ while $|\Top F|\cdot|F| = 15 > 14 = 2T(F)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:totalsize}
If $\Top F \notin F$ then $T(F^{+}) = T(F) + |\Top F|$ and $|F^{+}| = |F| + 1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[The averaging hypothesis survives the operation]\label{thm:stab}
If $|\Top F|\cdot|F| \le 2T(F)$ then $|\Top{F^{+}}|\cdot|F^{+}| \le 2T(F^{+})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:topstable} the top is unchanged; write $m := |\Top F|$. If $\Top F \in F$ then
$F^{+} = F$. Otherwise, by Lemma~\ref{lem:totalsize} the claim reads $m(|F|+1) \le 2T(F) + 2m$, i.e.\
$m|F| + m \le 2T(F) + 2m$, which follows from $m|F| \le 2T(F)$ together with $m \le 2m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
If $\Top F \ne \varnothing$ and $|\Top F|\cdot|F| \le 2T(F)$, then some $x \in \Top F$ is abundant in
$F^{+}$ --- obtainable either by transporting the witness of Theorem~\ref{thm:avg} through
Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, or by applying Theorem~\ref{thm:avg} to $F^{+}$ via Theorem~\ref{thm:stab}.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[A new top strictly increases density]\label{thm:density}
Suppose $F \ne \varnothing$, $\Top F \notin F$, $x \in \Top F$ and $\deeg_F(x) < |F|$. Then
$\deeg_F(x)/|F| < \deeg_{F^{+}}(x)/|F^{+}|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
With $d := \deeg_F(x)$ and $n := |F| > 0$ we have $\deeg_{F^{+}}(x) = d+1$ and $|F^{+}| = n+1$;
cross-multiplying, $d/n < (d+1)/(n+1)$ is equivalent to $d(n+1) < n(d+1)$, i.e.\ $d < n$.
\end{proof}

The excluded case is the trivial one: if $x$ lies in every member the density is already $1$.

\section{A sharp local degree law}

\begin{theorem}[Fibre bound]\label{thm:fibre}
Let $F$ be union-closed, $A \in F$ and $a \in A$. Then
$\#\{B \in F : a \notin B\} \le 2^{|A|-1}\deeg_F(a)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $G := \{B\in F : a \notin B\}$ and $\varphi(B) := B \cup A$. Union-closedness gives
$\varphi(B) \in F$, and $a \in A \subseteq \varphi(B)$, so $\varphi(G)$ is contained in the set of
members containing $a$ and $|\varphi(G)| \le \deeg_F(a)$. Fix $C \in \varphi(G)$; on the fibre
$\{B \in G : B \cup A = C\}$ the map $B \mapsto B \cap A$ is injective, because
$B = (C\setminus A)\cup (B\cap A)$ (every element of $B$ outside $A$ lies in $C \setminus A$, and
conversely $C\setminus A \subseteq B$ since $C = B \cup A$). Moreover $B \cap A \subseteq A\setminus\{a\}$
as $a \notin B$, so each fibre has at most $2^{|A|-1}$ elements. Hence
$|G| \le 2^{|A|-1}|\varphi(G)| \le 2^{|A|-1}\deeg_F(a)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Local degree bound]\label{thm:local}
Let $F$ be union-closed, $A \in F$, $a \in A$. Then $|F| \le (2^{|A|-1}+1)\deeg_F(a)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By \eqref{eq:partition}, $|F| = \deeg_F(a) + \#\{B : a \notin B\} \le \deeg_F(a) + 2^{|A|-1}\deeg_F(a)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Frankl's singleton case]\label{cor:singleton}
If a union-closed family contains $\{a\}$ then $a$ is abundant.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}
If a union-closed family contains $\{a,b\}$ with $a \ne b$ then $|F| \le 3\deeg_F(a)$ and
$|F| \le 3\deeg_F(b)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{definition}[Extremal family]
For $a \in A$ set $E(A,a) := \{A\} \cup \mathcal{P}(A\setminus\{a\})$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]\label{thm:sharp}
For every finite $A$ and $a \in A$, the family $E(A,a)$ is union-closed, contains $A$, and satisfies
$|E(A,a)| = (2^{|A|-1}+1)\deeg_{E(A,a)}(a)$. Hence the constant $2^{|A|-1}+1$ in
Theorem~\ref{thm:local} is optimal for every size of $A$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The union of two subsets of $A\setminus\{a\}$ is again one, and the union of $A$ with such a subset
is $A$; so $E(A,a)$ is union-closed. Only $A$ contains $a$, so the degree is $1$. Since
$A \notin \mathcal{P}(A\setminus\{a\})$ we get $|E(A,a)| = 2^{|A|-1}+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorems~\ref{thm:local} and \ref{thm:sharp} form an obstruction result: for $|A| = 1$ the law gives
abundance, but for $|A| \ge 2$ it gives at best $|F| \le 3\deeg$, and by sharpness no argument using
only the existence of a single member of size $k \ge 2$ can do better than the factor $2^{k-1}+1$.
Progress on Frankl's conjecture must use several members simultaneously.
\end{remark}

\section{Unconditional cases of Frankl's conjecture}

\begin{theorem}[Pair case]\label{thm:pair}
If $F$ is union-closed and $\{a,b\} \in F$, then $a$ or $b$ is abundant in $F$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $D_a, D_b$ be the members containing $a$, resp.\ $b$, let $N$ be the members containing neither,
and $I := D_a \cap D_b$. The map $B \mapsto B \cup \{a,b\}$ sends $N$ into $I$ by union-closedness and
is injective on $N$ (remove $a$ and $b$ to recover $B$), so $|N| \le |I|$. Inclusion--exclusion gives
$|D_a\cup D_b| + |I| = \deeg_F(a) + \deeg_F(b)$, and $|D_a \cup D_b| + |N| = |F|$; hence
$|F| \le \deeg_F(a) + \deeg_F(b)$. If neither element were abundant, doubling this inequality would
give $2|F| \le 2\deeg_F(a) + 2\deeg_F(b) < |F| + |F|$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Chains]\label{thm:chain}
Let $F$ be totally ordered by inclusion and have a nonempty member. Then $F$ is union-closed and has
an abundant element, whatever $|F|$ is.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The union of two comparable sets is the larger, so $F$ is union-closed. Let $S$ be the subfamily of
nonempty members, choose $M \in S$ of minimum cardinality, and pick $x \in M$. For $N \in S$,
comparability gives $M \subseteq N$ or $N \subseteq M$, and in the latter case minimality forces
$N = M$; either way $x \in N$. Hence $\deeg_F(x) \ge |S|$. At most one member is empty, so
$|S| \ge \max(1, |F|-1)$ and $2\deeg_F(x) \ge 2|S| \ge |S| + 1 \ge |F|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Small families]\label{thm:small}
Every union-closed family with a nonempty member and at most four members has an abundant element.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $|F| \le 2$, any $x$ in the nonempty member has $\deeg_F(x)\ge 1$ and $2 \ge |F|$. If
$|F| \in \{3,4\}$, at most one member is empty, so there are distinct nonempty $B \ne C$. If
$B \cup C = B$ then $C \subseteq B$ and any $x \in C$ lies in two members; otherwise $B \cup C$ is a
member distinct from $B$ and any $x \in B$ lies in two members. So $\deeg_F(x) \ge 2$ and
$2\deeg_F(x) \ge 4 \ge |F|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Three-element ground sets]
Every union-closed family over a three-element ground set with a nonempty member has an abundant
element; this is confirmed by exhaustive evaluation over all $2^{8} = 256$ families.
\end{theorem}

The standard exception $F = \{\varnothing\}$ is union-closed and nonempty yet has no abundant
element, which is why a nonempty member is required.

\begin{corollary}
For any family $F$ containing $\{a\}$ --- union-closed or not --- the element $a$ is abundant in
$\ucl(F)$ and remains abundant in $(\ucl(F))^{+}$; and if $\{a,b\} \in F$ then $a$ or $b$ is abundant
in $\ucl(F)$. Likewise the witnesses of Theorems~\ref{thm:chain} and \ref{thm:small} survive
adjoining the top.
\end{corollary}

\section{Algorithms and certificates}

\begin{theorem}[Single-maximum certificate]\label{thm:cert}
Let $s$ be a nonempty finite set. Then there exists $x \in s$ abundant in $F$ if and only if
$|F| \le 2\max_{x\in s}\deeg_F(x)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proposition}
Degrees are monotone in the family, so $\max_{x\in s}\deeg_F(x) \le \max_{x\in s}\deeg_{F^{+}}(x)$:
adjoining the top never decreases the certificate value. Consequently, if $s$ is nonempty and
$|F| \le 2\max_{x\in s}\deeg_F(x)$, then either $F = \varnothing$ or
$|F^{+}| \le 2\max_{x\in s}\deeg_{F^{+}}(x)$.
\end{proposition}

The certificate is an equivalence, not a shortcut: computing the maximum degree costs
$O(|F|\,|s|)$, linear in the family, but the family may be exponential in the ground set.

\paragraph{Procedures.} (1) \emph{Degree/surplus profile}: one pass over the incidence structure
yields all degrees and surpluses in $O(|F|\,|s|)$ time; Theorem~\ref{thm:cert} answers the existence
question from the maximum. (2) \emph{Adjoin-the-top audit}: compute $\Top F$, test membership, and
predict the new profile from Lemma~\ref{lem:unit} without recomputation --- every element occurring
in $F$ gains $+1$. (3) \emph{Averaging test}: compute $T(F)$ and check $|s|\,|F| \le 2T(F)$; if it
passes, a degree maximiser is an abundant element. (4) \emph{Closure with a surplus ledger}: iterate
pairwise completion to stabilisation, recording $+1$ or $-1$ at each adjunction for a tracked
element; the resulting lattice path is the object of Direction 1 below.

\section{Discussion}

The opening question --- does adjoining the top preserve an abundant witness, and does the parity of
$|F|$ falsify it? --- resolves cleanly: the claim is true, parity is a red herring, and the unique
failure is the empty family. The reason is the two-versus-one accounting of Lemma~\ref{lem:unit},
which also explains Theorem~\ref{thm:main} (the top is a $+1$ batch), predicts
Theorem~\ref{thm:sharpclosure} (the pairwise step adds a $-1$ set), quantifies the cost of any batch
(Corollary~\ref{cor:batch}), and refines to a scale-free statement
(Theorem~\ref{thm:density}). Independently, the incidence-matrix viewpoint yields a creation
mechanism, the averaging criterion of Theorem~\ref{thm:avg}, which requires no union-closedness and
is itself preserved by the same operation (Theorem~\ref{thm:stab}). The consistency of the two
mechanisms reflects the fact that degrees and total size are the column and row marginals of one
matrix, and adjoining the top appends a row maximal on the support.

None of these results proves Frankl's conjecture or reduces its complexity: the averaging criterion
is silent on sparse families (Proposition~\ref{prop:notnec}), the local degree law is provably
unimprovable (Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}), the certificate does not shrink the search space, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:sharpclosure} shows the closure can destroy any specific witness. What the paper
contributes is an exact calculus for the one operation that is always safe, a precise identification
of where safety ends, and a quantitative obstruction that redirects effort away from single-member
arguments.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{Direction 1: batch-surplus schedules for the union closure.}
The closure can be surplus-negative, but it is reached through many single adjunctions, each worth
exactly $+1$ or $-1$. \emph{Conjecture: for every union-closed target there is an ordering of the
added sets along which the running surplus of some fixed element never drops below its starting
value.} Adjoining the top is safe because it is the batch of size one with positive surplus, so the
question is not whether a batch is dangerous but whether it can be scheduled into safe increments.
Surplus additivity makes the schedule a lattice path, testable exhaustively on small ground sets.

\paragraph{Direction 2: the $2^{k-1}+1$ law as a Frankl obstruction.}
\emph{Conjecture: for a union-closed family in which every nonempty member has size at least $k$, the
best possible abundance ratio degrades exactly like $2^{k-1}+1$, and $E(A,a)$ is the unique
extremiser up to isomorphism.} The extremal family is a power set with a single cap, so extremality
is a statement about how much of a Boolean lattice can sit below one cap; only uniqueness is missing.

\paragraph{Direction 3: structured families beyond chains and small sizes.}
Chains and families of at most four members are the two extremes of the same counting. Families of
bounded width in the inclusion order, families generated by few sets, and families with a bounded
number of maximal members are the natural next targets, with the surplus ledger measuring the
distance to the threshold.

\paragraph{Direction 4: quantitative averaging.}
Theorem~\ref{thm:avg} is a threshold statement. What is the best abundance guarantee obtainable as a
function of the average member density $T(F)/(|s|\,|F|)$?

